1) HTTP - stateless, Request-Response protocol, plain text
Client Request:
* GET - Retrieves data from server
* POST - Sends data to update or create a resource
* PUT - Replaces an entire resource in server
* DELETE - Removes a resource from server
* OPTIONS - Asks the server which HTTP methods are allowed for specific URL
* HEAD - Identical to GET only return the header not body
Server's Reply:
* 2XX(Success) - 200 OK = successful, 201 Created = new resource created successfully
* 3XX(Redirection) - 301 Moved Permanently = Resource has new URL, 302 Found = Temporary Redirect
* 4XX(Client Error) - 400 Bad Request = The server couldn't understand your request, 401 Unauthorized = Authentication is required, 403 Forbidden = Authenticated but don't have access permission to access the resources, 404 Not Found = Resource doesn't exist.
* 5XX(Server Error) - 500 Internal Server = Server crashed when processing your request, 502 Bad Gateway = Invalid Response from the backend, 503 Service Unavailable = Server is overload or under maintenance
Exploit:
* If the server's access controls is misconfigured, attackers use Burp Suite to change GET request to PUT request
2) HTTPS - TLS handshake before data is send, certificate verification and Encrypted Communication - all request and reply are encrypted
Exploit:
* SSL Stripping - If website supports HTTP and HTTPS attacker intercept the connection and downgrades to HTTP results in no padlock in victim browser.
Defender:
* HSTS(HTTP Strict Transport Security) - forces browser to HTTPS

3) FTP(File Transfer Protocol) - designed for uploading and downloading files between client and a server
* port 21 Control Channel(used to send commands), port 20 Data Channel(used to transfer files)
* uses plain text authentication, Plain text data and Two port complexity(nightmare to configure through firewall)
Exploit:
* FTP Credential Harvesting - attackers sniff plaintext credentials on network
Prevention:
* FTPS(FTP Secure) - FTP wrapped in TLS/SSL encryption
4) SMB(Server Message Block) - Windows File Sharing Protocol - works on port 445 over TCP and on port 139 over NetBIOS
* allows users to access files, printers and serial ports on remote computers
Exploit:
* Lateral Movement - When a single system is compromised in a network they use SMB to access shared folders, to login other machines moving laterally between network.
* EternalBlue and WannaCry - exploited SMBv1 enabled attackers to execute arbitrary code on unpatched Windows machine without credentials itself.
* NTLM Relay Attacks - Windows automatically sends the credentials in form of NTLM hashes to authenticate, attackers uses "Responder" to send that NTLM hashes to them and crack that hash offline.
Prevention:
* Disable SMBv1 use SMBv2 or SMBv3
* Block port 445 to public internet.
* Enable SMB Signaling - digitally signs every SMB packet to prevent MITM attacks
* Provide least privilege on shared folders

5) SMTP(Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) - push protocol
* port 25(Server-to-Server relay), port 587(Client-to-Server Submission with encryption), port 465(Legacy SMTPS)
Steps:
* Email sent to organization's STMP server - fetch reciptant domain - quires DNS for Mail Exchange(MX) records - relays the message to Google's SMTP server
Flaws: 
* No built in mechanism to verify "From" address
* If the STMP server is misconfigured as open relay, it will accept and send mails from anyone on the internet leading to blacklist of the server.
6) POP3 (Post Office Protocol version 3)
* retrieves message to local and deletes in server.
* only the first accessed device does have the message.
* No sync exists - Port 110 (unencrypted) and port 995 (Encrypted via SSL/TLS)
7) IMAP (Internet Message Access Protocol)
* retrieves message to local and exists in server as read.
* multiple devices have the message by sync exists between devices
* Port 143 (unencrypted) , Port 993(encrypted via SSL/TLS)
Exploit:
* attackers uses Wireshark on local network to sniff on port 110 and 143
Prevention:
* can be prevented by forcing port 995 and 993 rather than 110 and 143

8) LDAP (Lightweight Directory Access Protocol)
directory service - database that stores and manage network users, devices and resources - In windows Active Directory(AD) database and LDAP is the language to communicate
* used to read and modify directory service - Port 389 (unencrypted) and port 636 (Encrypted via LDAPS)
* data is stored in hierarchical tree structure - folders = OUs(Organizational Units) - files = Objects(have unique name called Distinguished Name(DN))
Exploit:
* LDAP injection - hacker target AD with unsanitised user input to bypass authentication
* If machine compromised within corporation network, attackers query LDAP to perform LDAP enumeration to map entire organization without admin privileges.
* Attacker can extract complete list of usernames - password policies - organizational structure
9) Kerberos - Port 88 (TCP and UDP) - Authenticator 
NTLM - older Windows Authentication protocol for active directory(AD), sends password hashes back and forth on the network to authenticate which can be intercepted and cracked.
* Kerberos is complex and highly secure protocol that relays on tickets and asymmetric cryptography.
Methods:
* AS-REQ/AS-REP: KDC(Key Distribution Centre) authenticates user using password hash. KDC verifies and issues TGT(Ticket Granting Ticket) which is encrypted with secret key known only by KDC.
* TGS-REQ/TGS-REP: If user needs specific service, they present the TGT, KDC validates and issues ST(Service Ticket) to that particular service.
* AP-REQ/AP-REP: User presents the Service Ticket to target server and the server decrypts, verifies user and grant access.
Exploit:
service accounts - service accounts in Access Directory is a non-human account created to run background applications, services or scripts.
* Kerberoasting - Every service has service accounts, when attacker request server ticket KDC generates ticket and encrypts it using NTLM password hash of the the service account, the attacker cracks it offline.
Prevention:
* service accounts must have complex, randomly generated passwords(25+ characters)
